<article class="content">
<h1>How to measure goalie pads for the right size</h1>
<p>Every current manufacturer sizes leg pads from a leg measurement, but they do not all ask for the same one. Bauer, CCM, TRUE and Warrior charts key on <strong>floor-to-knee (FTK)</strong>; Brian's keys on <strong>ankle-to-knee (ATK)</strong> plus a knee-to-thigh measurement. The single rule that matters: measure the exact line the brand's chart names, and never convert one method into another.</p>
<section class="card"><h2>The short version</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Bauer, CCM, TRUE, Warrior:</strong> measure floor-to-knee — from the floor straight up to the centre of the kneecap, in bare feet or socks, knee slightly bent.</li>
<li><strong>Brian's:</strong> measure ankle-to-knee — from the ankle bone to the centre of the kneecap with the leg slightly bent — plus knee to desired thigh height.</li>
<li><strong>Vaughn:</strong> publishes no measuring method and directs buyers to an in-person retailer fitting.</li>
</ul>
<p>FTK includes your foot and ankle height, so it is always several inches longer than ATK. Feeding an ATK value into an FTK chart (or the reverse) produces a pad one or more sizes wrong.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2>What you need</h2>
<ul>
<li>A flexible tape measure marked in inches or centimetres. A piece of string measured afterwards against a rigid ruler also works.</li>
<li>A wall or firm chair, depending on whether the chart describes a standing or seated measurement.</li>
<li>A helper to hold the tape straight and read the endpoint — self-measuring bends the tape.</li>
</ul></section>
<section class="card"><h2>Floor-to-knee (Bauer, CCM, TRUE, Warrior)</h2>
<ol>
<li>Remove skates and shoes. Measure in bare feet or thin socks on a flat floor.</li>
<li>Stand with feet shoulder-width apart and a slight, relaxed bend in the knee. (Warrior's official chart diagram shows this measurement taken while sitting — follow the posture your brand's chart describes.)</li>
<li>Find the centre of the kneecap — not its top or bottom edge.</li>
<li>Run the tape vertically from the floor straight up to that centre point. Do not follow the curve of the shin.</li>
<li>Record the value with its unit, then measure once more to confirm.</li>
</ol>
<p>CCM and Warrior charts also use your standing height next to floor-to-knee, so record height too. CCM additionally publishes a seated tibia-length alternative measured with the skate on; our reviewed CCM rows use the floor-to-knee and height columns of the published chart.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2>Ankle-to-knee and knee-to-thigh (Brian's)</h2>
<ol>
<li>With the leg slightly bent, place the tape at the ankle bone.</li>
<li>Measure straight up to the centre of the kneecap. That is ATK.</li>
<li>Then measure from the centre of the kneecap up to your desired thigh height. Brian's chart pairs each stock size with an ATK value and a thigh-rise length.</li>
<li>Record both values and their units together.</li>
</ol></section>
<section class="card"><h2>What each brand's chart actually asks for</h2>
<p class="table-hint" id="method-scroll-hint">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" aria-describedby="method-scroll-hint"><table>
<caption>Reviewed measurement methods by brand</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Brand</th><th scope="col">Chart input</th><th scope="col">Published measuring detail</th><th scope="col">Also required</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Bauer</th><td>Floor-to-knee (“shin height”)</td><td>Soft tape from the floor to the centre of the knee.</td><td>Age/level category (Youth–Senior)</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">CCM</th><td>Floor-to-knee</td><td>Bare foot on the floor, straight up to the middle of the kneecap, knee slightly bent.</td><td>Standing height</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">TRUE</th><td>Floor-to-knee</td><td>Bare feet, slight knee bend, tape to the middle of the knee.</td><td>Level category (JR/INT/SR)</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Warrior</th><td>Floor-to-knee</td><td>Official chart diagram: taken while sitting, floor to knee pivot point.</td><td>Standing height; thigh measurement for the “+” extension</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Brian's</th><td>Ankle-to-knee</td><td>Leg slightly bent, ankle bone to the centre of the knee.</td><td>Knee-to-thigh length</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Vaughn</th><td>None published</td><td>Vaughn directs buyers to an in-person retailer fitting.</td><td>—</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>The same measurement maps to different sizes across brands — a 19.5 in floor-to-knee lands in different rows on the Bauer, CCM, Warrior and TRUE charts. That is why the <a href="/calculator">calculator</a> asks for your brand first and never reuses a number across chart systems.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2>About “ATK × 2” and “ATK + skate size + thigh” formulas</h2>
<p>Older retailer guides estimate pad size by doubling ATK, or by adding ATK, skate size and a thigh measurement. These are rules of thumb, not any current manufacturer's published method — none of the six brands above instructs an arithmetic step like that. A formula estimate can be a sanity check, but the size decision should come from the brand's own chart row and, above all, from the on-ice knee-block check.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2>Common mistakes</h2>
<ul>
<li>Measuring to the top or bottom of the kneecap instead of its centre.</li>
<li>Following the curve of the shin rather than the straight vertical line.</li>
<li>Measuring floor-to-knee with skates or shoes on.</li>
<li>Using an ATK value in a floor-to-knee chart, or converting between the two with a guessed offset.</li>
<li>Sizing from standing height or age alone.</li>
<li>Buying oversized pads “to grow into” beyond the knee-cradle limit — oversize pads rotate poorly and punish developing technique.</li>
<li>Cranking every strap down to force a wrong size to feel right. If a pad only fits when over-tightened, the size or model is wrong.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>No tape?</strong> Do not estimate from height or age. Wait until you can measure, or ask a qualified retailer/fitter to measure.</p></section>
